## Why This Is Asked

Assesses CKNE-driven multi-tenant graph analytics design, focusing on per-tenant prioritization, adaptive traversal, caching, and safe degradation under load. Requires concrete parameters, fairness guarantees, observability hooks, and a test plan.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-routing and depth mapping
- Backpressure and resource reallocation
- Incremental caching and warm/cold budgets
- Telemetry, SLAs, and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
function adjustForCKNE(tenantCKNE, req) {
  const baseDepth = 6;
  if (tenantCKNE < 0.3) {
    req.depth = Math.min(req.depth || baseDepth, 3);
    req.rateLimit = Math.min(req.rateLimit || 200, 100);
  } else if (tenantCKNE > 0.7) {
    req.depth = Math.max(req.depth || baseDepth, 8);
    req.cacheHint = 'warm';
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the degradation loop under bursty CKNE distributions?
- How would you ensure fairness when CKNE changes dynamically?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical multi-tenant QoS design, CKNE-to-RESOURCE mapping, and a simple yet effective rebalancing loop for a FaaS platform.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-concurrency mapping for tenants
- Baseline SLA and fairness guarantees
- Minute-by-minute rebalancing logic
- Telemetry, metrics, and observability
- Practical payload handling and testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch: compute per-tenant quotas given totalCap and ckne
function rebalance(tenants, totalCap) {
  // simple proportional allocation based on CKNE, with floor baseline
  const sumCKNE = tenants.reduce((s,t)=>s+t.ckne,0);
  return tenants.map(t => {
    const p = t.ckne / sumCKNE;
    return {tenant: t.tenant, quota: Math.max(1, Math.round(p * totalCap))};
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure and guarantee tail latency under bursty traffic?
- How would you extend this to handle new tenants joining mid-cycle?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical CKNE-driven canary rollout design for multi-tenant systems, combining edge decision loops with per-tenant state and SLA awareness.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE health integration at edge per tenant
- Canary rollout policy with minute-by-minute loop
- Per-tenant routing and telemetry payloads
- Safety and rollback criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// minute-by-minute loop (high level)
for (const tenant of tenants) {
  const healthy = health[tenant].latency < 150 && health[tenant].errors < 0.005;
  if (healthy && rollout[tenant] < 40) rollout[tenant] += 5;
  else if (!healthy) rollout[tenant] = 5;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate the policy with synthetic canary tests?
- What metrics would you alert on for degraded tenants?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design CKNE-aware scheduling for data pipelines, balancing fairness and performance under multi-tenant load.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-slot mapping and data-freshness deadlines
- Minute-by-minute rebalancing with weighted fairness
- Baseline SLA guarantees and per-tenant minimums
- Telemetry: queue depths, latencies, CKNE correlations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: compute per-tenant slot allocation from CKNE and queue depth
function allocateSlots(tenants, totalSlots, baseline) {
  const weights = tenants.map(t => t.ckne * A + (baseline - t.queue) / baseline * B);
  const sum = weights.reduce((x, y) => x + y, 0);
  return tenants.map((t, i) => Math.max(t.minSlots, Math.floor((weights[i] / sum) * totalSlots)));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for tenant churn or abrupt CKNE shifts?
- What metrics would you expose to observe fairness and SLA adherence?
